1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,028
In this video, we'll learn how coordinates
flatten our round Earth into maps,

2
00:00:05,028 --> 00:00:07,853
and see how they're used
to draw weather maps,

3
00:00:07,853 --> 00:00:10,800
and to read the winds
high above the North Pole.

4
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:15,538
Why does Greenland look as big
as Africa on so many maps,

5
00:00:15,538 --> 00:00:19,475
and how do forecasters draw
the whole Arctic on one page?

6
00:00:19,825 --> 00:00:23,275
This is the last of seven
parts on coordinates.

7
00:00:23,625 --> 00:00:26,206
Now let's dive into the maps themselves:

8
00:00:26,206 --> 00:00:30,206
the flat square map that weather
data come on, Mercator's map,

9
00:00:30,206 --> 00:00:32,787
where the rhumb line comes out straight,

10
00:00:32,787 --> 00:00:37,175
and maps that keep areas true, with
exactly what each one stretches.

11
00:00:37,525 --> 00:00:41,925
Then let's read a weather map
drawn from above the North Pole.

12
00:00:42,725 --> 00:00:46,500
Every map starts by flattening
the Earth onto a page.

13
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:51,775
And here's the catch: you can't flatten
a sphere without stretching or tearing it.

14
00:00:52,125 --> 00:00:54,325
Try it with an orange peel.

15
00:00:54,675 --> 00:00:57,493
Cut it into slices from pole to pole,

16
00:00:57,493 --> 00:01:01,225
and lay them flat, and gaps
open up between them.

17
00:01:01,575 --> 00:01:05,225
Close the gaps by stretching,
and the shapes change.

18
00:01:05,575 --> 00:01:09,850
Every flat map of the Earth
makes a choice about what to stretch.

19
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:15,175
Let's build the simplest map, and work
out exactly how much it stretches.

20
00:01:15,975 --> 00:01:21,025
Next, we'll build the simplest map
step by step, with every number.

21
00:01:21,375 --> 00:01:24,425
That's for anyone who enjoys every step.

22
00:01:24,775 --> 00:01:30,652
For the intuition behind weather, climate
science or GIS, you only need the result,

23
00:01:30,652 --> 00:01:34,900
so if it feels like a lot, skip
ahead to the time on screen.

24
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:39,475
We'll build it from the lines
on the globe whose lengths we know.

25
00:01:39,825 --> 00:01:41,875
First, the equator.

26
00:01:42,225 --> 00:01:46,325
Cut it, and unroll it
into a straight line across the page.

27
00:01:46,675 --> 00:01:49,864
Its length is two
pi times the Earth's radius,

28
00:01:49,864 --> 00:01:53,053
which, as in Part 3, we
write as the letter a,

29
00:01:53,053 --> 00:01:57,212
about six thousand three hundred
and seventy one kilometres,

30
00:01:57,212 --> 00:02:01,025
so the equator is about forty
thousand kilometres long.

31
00:02:01,375 --> 00:02:04,153
A place at longitude lambda, in radians,

32
00:02:04,153 --> 00:02:09,500
is the Earth's radius times lambda along
the equator from the prime meridian.

33
00:02:09,850 --> 00:02:13,500
That's the arc rule, which
we found in Part 1.

34
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:17,850
So we set x equal to the Earth's
radius times lambda.

35
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:21,375
Second, the meridians.

36
00:02:21,725 --> 00:02:24,695
Each one runs
from the equator to the pole,

37
00:02:24,695 --> 00:02:30,013
and a place at latitude phi is the Earth's
radius times phi from the equator,

38
00:02:30,013 --> 00:02:31,325
along its meridian.

39
00:02:31,675 --> 00:02:35,575
That's the distance
north, which we found in Part 3.

40
00:02:35,925 --> 00:02:40,239
So we draw every meridian
as a straight line going up the page,

41
00:02:40,239 --> 00:02:43,525
and set y equal to the Earth's
radius times phi.

42
00:02:45,025 --> 00:02:46,111
Let's try Brest,

43
00:02:46,111 --> 00:02:50,525
the port city at the western tip
of France that we met in Part 3,

44
00:02:50,525 --> 00:02:55,550
at forty eight point four degrees
north, and four and a half degrees west.

45
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:59,691
In radians, that's
zero point eight four four seven,

46
00:02:59,691 --> 00:03:02,825
and minus
zero point zero seven eight five.

47
00:03:03,175 --> 00:03:06,000
So x, the Earth's radius times lambda,

48
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,575
comes to about minus five hundred
kilometres: just west of the middle line.

49
00:03:13,075 --> 00:03:15,578
And y, the Earth's radius times phi,

50
00:03:15,578 --> 00:03:20,375
comes to about five thousand three
hundred and eighty two kilometres.

51
00:03:21,875 --> 00:03:27,800
That's exactly how far Brest is from the
equator, walking north along its meridian.

52
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:33,450
So along the equator, and along every
meridian, this map is true to scale.

53
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:37,252
It's called the plate
carrée, French for flat square,

54
00:03:37,252 --> 00:03:40,900
and most weather data come
on exactly this kind of grid.

55
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:45,450
Now look at a circle of latitude,
called a parallel.

56
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:50,937
On this map, every parallel is drawn
as a straight line across the page,

57
00:03:50,937 --> 00:03:52,578
as long as the equator:

58
00:03:52,578 --> 00:03:58,000
two pi times the Earth's radius, about
forty thousand and thirty kilometres.

59
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:02,900
But on the globe, the parallel
through Brest is a smaller circle.

60
00:04:03,250 --> 00:04:08,603
As we saw in Part 3, its radius
is the Earth's radius times cosine phi,

61
00:04:08,603 --> 00:04:12,675
about four thousand two
hundred and thirty kilometres.

62
00:04:13,025 --> 00:04:16,152
So its true length, two
pi times that radius,

63
00:04:16,152 --> 00:04:20,600
is about twenty six thousand five
hundred and eighty kilometres.

64
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:26,037
The map draws twenty six
thousand five hundred and eighty

65
00:04:26,037 --> 00:04:28,800
kilometres as forty thousand and thirty.

66
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:32,239
So east-west distances
at Brest are stretched:

67
00:04:32,239 --> 00:04:37,075
dividing the drawn length by the true
length gives about one point five.

68
00:04:38,575 --> 00:04:40,850
Let's write that with symbols.

69
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:45,476
The stretch, called k, is two
pi times the Earth's radius,

70
00:04:45,476 --> 00:04:49,900
divided by two pi times the Earth's
radius times cosine phi.

71
00:04:50,250 --> 00:04:55,825
The two pi and the radius
cancel, so k equals one over cosine phi.

72
00:04:57,325 --> 00:05:01,663
At Brest, the cosine of phi
is zero point six six four,

73
00:05:01,663 --> 00:05:03,950
so k is about one point five.

74
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:09,775
At sixty degrees north, the cosine
is zero point five, so k is two.

75
00:05:10,125 --> 00:05:15,410
And at Longyearbyen, the Arctic town
on Svalbard that we met in Part 3,

76
00:05:15,410 --> 00:05:18,462
at seventy eight point two degrees north,

77
00:05:18,462 --> 00:05:23,300
the cosine is only zero point two
zero four, so k is almost five.

78
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:28,100
North-south distances
aren't stretched at all.

79
00:05:28,450 --> 00:05:32,333
So a small circle near Brest
is drawn as an oval, one and

80
00:05:32,333 --> 00:05:37,850
a half times wider than it is tall, and
near the poles, shapes are squashed flat.

81
00:05:38,650 --> 00:05:41,325
If you skipped, here's what to keep.

82
00:05:41,675 --> 00:05:46,400
The simplest map uses longitude
and latitude as x and y.

83
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:50,473
It's true along the equator
and along every meridian,

84
00:05:50,473 --> 00:05:54,688
but it stretches east-west
distances by one over cosine phi:

85
00:05:54,688 --> 00:05:57,216
about one and a half times at Brest,

86
00:05:57,216 --> 00:06:01,150
two at sixty degrees
north, and almost five at Svalbard.

87
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:04,636
In Part 6, we called
the distance walked for

88
00:06:04,636 --> 00:06:07,844
each radian of a coordinate
its scale factor:

89
00:06:07,844 --> 00:06:13,475
the Earth's radius for latitude, and the
radius times cosine phi for longitude.

90
00:06:13,825 --> 00:06:16,958
Scale factors like this
matter for weather data,

91
00:06:16,958 --> 00:06:20,875
because most weather data come
on exactly this kind of grid.

92
00:06:21,225 --> 00:06:25,943
Suppose two grid points near Brest
are one degree of longitude apart,

93
00:06:25,943 --> 00:06:30,250
and the temperature differs by one
degree Celsius between them.

94
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:34,300
The slope we want is per kilometre,
not per degree.

95
00:06:34,650 --> 00:06:39,864
So we divide by the true distance east,
seventy three point eight kilometres,

96
00:06:39,864 --> 00:06:42,098
as we found in Part 3, not by the

97
00:06:42,098 --> 00:06:46,025
hundred and eleven point two
kilometres of a degree north.

98
00:06:46,375 --> 00:06:50,525
That's about one point four
degrees per hundred kilometres.

99
00:06:52,025 --> 00:06:57,950
In symbols, the eastward slope is one
over the Earth's radius times cosine phi,

100
00:06:57,950 --> 00:07:00,350
times the change with longitude.

101
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:05,250
Together with the northward slope, it
makes the gradient on the sphere.

102
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:10,250
We'll build it step by step in the next
movie in this series: The gradient.

103
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:14,982
Weather models also use a curvilinear
coordinate in the vertical,

104
00:07:14,982 --> 00:07:18,758
pressure instead of height, which
has its own movie too:

105
00:07:18,758 --> 00:07:21,050
Pressure as a vertical coordinate.

106
00:07:21,850 --> 00:07:23,382
In fifteen sixty nine,

107
00:07:23,382 --> 00:07:26,794
the map maker Gerardus
Mercator had a clever idea

108
00:07:26,794 --> 00:07:30,275
to fix the squashed shapes
of the flat square map.

109
00:07:30,625 --> 00:07:32,825
Think of stretching a photo.

110
00:07:33,175 --> 00:07:37,250
If you stretch it only sideways,
faces look too wide.

111
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:41,441
But if you also stretch it up
and down by the same amount,

112
00:07:41,441 --> 00:07:43,825
faces look right again, just bigger.

113
00:07:44,175 --> 00:07:48,750
Mercator did this to the map, one
strip of latitude at a time.

114
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:52,831
Near Brest, east-west
is stretched by one point five,

115
00:07:52,831 --> 00:07:57,125
so he stretched north-south near
Brest by one point five too.

116
00:07:57,475 --> 00:08:00,825
Near sixty degrees north, both by two.

117
00:08:01,175 --> 00:08:04,350
Near Svalbard, both by almost five.

118
00:08:05,850 --> 00:08:08,775
Now watch a small circle from the globe.

119
00:08:09,125 --> 00:08:13,150
On the flat square map, it
becomes an oval, too wide.

120
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:16,802
Stretch its strip
north-south by the same factor,

121
00:08:16,802 --> 00:08:19,700
and it becomes a circle
again, only bigger.

122
00:08:20,050 --> 00:08:24,758
Unlike a photo, though, each strip
of latitude needs its own stretch,

123
00:08:24,758 --> 00:08:28,375
so the map grows taller
and taller towards the poles.

124
00:08:28,725 --> 00:08:33,611
Because small shapes stay the right
shape, small angles stay true as well:

125
00:08:33,611 --> 00:08:34,799
the angle at which

126
00:08:34,799 --> 00:08:39,025
a road crosses a meridian on the map
is the angle on the ground.

127
00:08:39,375 --> 00:08:41,667
Maps like this are called conformal,

128
00:08:41,667 --> 00:08:46,250
which means that they keep shapes
and angles right in every small patch.

129
00:08:47,050 --> 00:08:52,900
Now let's turn Mercator's idea into a
formula, with every step and every number.

130
00:08:53,250 --> 00:08:57,485
This part is for learners who want
to see how the formula is built;

131
00:08:57,485 --> 00:09:02,225
you don't need it for the intuition
behind weather, climate science or GIS.

132
00:09:02,575 --> 00:09:06,775
If it feels like too much, skip
ahead to the time on screen.

133
00:09:07,125 --> 00:09:11,100
Take a small step north
on the globe, by delta phi.

134
00:09:11,450 --> 00:09:16,525
From Part 3, its true length
is the Earth's radius times delta phi.

135
00:09:16,875 --> 00:09:20,489
On the flat square
map, it's drawn just that long,

136
00:09:20,489 --> 00:09:24,825
while east-west is drawn
one over cosine phi times too long.

137
00:09:25,175 --> 00:09:26,545
To keep the shapes,

138
00:09:26,545 --> 00:09:31,375
Mercator draws the north step
one over cosine phi times longer too.

139
00:09:31,725 --> 00:09:37,260
So on his map, the step is drawn
as the Earth's radius times delta phi,

140
00:09:37,260 --> 00:09:38,975
divided by cosine phi.

141
00:09:40,475 --> 00:09:42,425
Let's put in numbers.

142
00:09:42,775 --> 00:09:44,332
One degree north is a

143
00:09:44,332 --> 00:09:49,225
hundred and eleven point two
kilometres on the ground, everywhere.

144
00:09:49,575 --> 00:09:53,450
At Brest, where the stretch
is one point five zero six,

145
00:09:53,450 --> 00:09:58,875
Mercator draws it about a hundred and
sixty seven point four kilometres long.

146
00:10:00,375 --> 00:10:03,875
At sixty degrees north, he
draws it twice as long,

147
00:10:03,875 --> 00:10:07,725
about two hundred and twenty
two point four kilometres.

148
00:10:09,225 --> 00:10:14,200
When the step is tiny, we
write d instead of delta, as in Part 3.

149
00:10:14,550 --> 00:10:20,325
So d y equals the Earth's
radius times d phi, divided by cosine phi.

150
00:10:20,675 --> 00:10:24,644
This says how fast the map's
height grows as we go north:

151
00:10:24,644 --> 00:10:29,100
slowly near the equator,
and faster and faster towards the pole.

152
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:33,550
So how high up the map
is a place at latitude phi?

153
00:10:33,900 --> 00:10:38,300
It's the sum of all the drawn
steps, from the equator up to phi.

154
00:10:38,650 --> 00:10:41,825
Let's add them up, ten degrees at a time.

155
00:10:42,175 --> 00:10:46,750
Ten degrees
is zero point one seven four five radians.

156
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:50,925
For each step, we use the stretch
in the middle of the step.

157
00:10:51,275 --> 00:10:55,211
From zero to ten degrees,
the middle is five degrees,

158
00:10:55,211 --> 00:10:58,925
where one over cosine
is one point zero zero four.

159
00:10:59,275 --> 00:11:03,967
So the first drawn step, ten degrees
in radians times that stretch,

160
00:11:03,967 --> 00:11:07,258
is about zero point one seven five
Earth radii,

161
00:11:07,258 --> 00:11:11,250
or about one thousand one
hundred and sixteen kilometres.

162
00:11:12,750 --> 00:11:16,720
From ten to twenty degrees,
the middle is fifteen degrees,

163
00:11:16,720 --> 00:11:22,400
the stretch is one point zero three five,
and the step is zero point one eight one.

164
00:11:22,750 --> 00:11:26,225
The running total
is zero point three five six.

165
00:11:27,725 --> 00:11:32,644
From twenty to thirty degrees,
the stretch is one point one zero three,

166
00:11:32,644 --> 00:11:38,325
the step is zero point one nine three, and
the total is zero point five four nine.

167
00:11:39,825 --> 00:11:41,550
Let's speed up.

168
00:11:41,900 --> 00:11:46,084
The total reaches about
one point zero one at fifty degrees,

169
00:11:46,084 --> 00:11:48,107
one point three one at sixty,

170
00:11:48,107 --> 00:11:52,500
one point seven three at seventy,
and two point four at eighty.

171
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,600
Look at the steps: they grow as we
go north, because the stretch grows.

172
00:11:58,950 --> 00:12:01,691
The last ten degrees,
from eighty to ninety,

173
00:12:01,691 --> 00:12:06,550
add at least two more on their own, and
we'll see soon that they add far more.

174
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:10,824
Navigators really did it this
way: in fifteen ninety nine,

175
00:12:10,824 --> 00:12:13,463
the English mathematician Edward Wright

176
00:12:13,463 --> 00:12:16,642
published a table
made by adding up such steps,

177
00:12:16,642 --> 00:12:18,875
one minute of latitude at a time.

178
00:12:19,675 --> 00:12:24,901
Adding up tiny steps like this
is the integration we met in Part 3,

179
00:12:24,901 --> 00:12:27,475
and this sum has an exact answer.

180
00:12:27,825 --> 00:12:32,975
To write it, we need one new
tool, the natural logarithm, written l n.

181
00:12:33,325 --> 00:12:36,750
The number e is about
two point seven one eight.

182
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:41,071
The natural logarithm
of a number answers one question:

183
00:12:41,071 --> 00:12:43,525
e to what power gives this number?

184
00:12:43,875 --> 00:12:48,625
So l n of one is zero,
because e to the power zero is one.

185
00:12:48,975 --> 00:12:51,150
And l n of e is one.

186
00:12:52,650 --> 00:12:56,770
Here's the answer to our sum:
y equals the Earth's radius,

187
00:12:56,770 --> 00:12:59,967
times the natural
logarithm of the tangent of

188
00:12:59,967 --> 00:13:02,950
forty five degrees plus half the latitude.

189
00:13:03,300 --> 00:13:06,595
Forty five degrees
is pi over four radians,

190
00:13:06,595 --> 00:13:10,350
so on screen it's pi over four
plus phi over two.

191
00:13:10,700 --> 00:13:13,275
Let's test it against our table.

192
00:13:13,625 --> 00:13:14,881
At sixty degrees,

193
00:13:14,881 --> 00:13:20,350
adding half the latitude to forty five
degrees gives seventy five degrees.

194
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:23,738
Its tangent
is three point seven three two,

195
00:13:23,738 --> 00:13:27,200
and the l n of that
is one point three one seven.

196
00:13:28,700 --> 00:13:33,225
Our ten-degree sum gave
one point three one three, very close.

197
00:13:33,575 --> 00:13:38,178
With one-degree steps, the sum
gives one point three one seven,

198
00:13:38,178 --> 00:13:40,150
the same to three decimals.

199
00:13:41,650 --> 00:13:44,678
At Brest, forty five degrees plus half its

200
00:13:44,678 --> 00:13:47,850
latitude gives sixty
nine point two degrees.

201
00:13:48,200 --> 00:13:51,448
Its tangent
is two point six three two five,

202
00:13:51,448 --> 00:13:54,475
and its l n is zero point nine six eight.

203
00:13:54,825 --> 00:13:56,307
So on a Mercator map,

204
00:13:56,307 --> 00:14:01,033
Brest sits zero point nine six eight
Earth radii above the equator,

205
00:14:01,033 --> 00:14:05,125
about six thousand one hundred
and sixty seven kilometres.

206
00:14:06,625 --> 00:14:11,375
On the flat square map, it was five
thousand three hundred and eighty two.

207
00:14:12,175 --> 00:14:17,275
How can we be sure the formula is right
everywhere, not just at a few points?

208
00:14:17,625 --> 00:14:23,600
Its slope must match the drawn step: one
over cosine phi for each radian of phi.

209
00:14:23,950 --> 00:14:26,944
Let's check its slope
at sixty degrees with the

210
00:14:26,944 --> 00:14:29,875
centred difference
from the derivatives movie.

211
00:14:30,225 --> 00:14:35,350
At sixty one degrees, the formula
gives one point three five two four.

212
00:14:35,700 --> 00:14:40,000
At fifty nine degrees, it gives
one point two eight two six.

213
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:44,743
The difference
is zero point zero six nine eight,

214
00:14:44,743 --> 00:14:47,457
and the two points are two degrees apart,

215
00:14:47,457 --> 00:14:50,700
which is zero point zero
three four nine radians.

216
00:14:51,050 --> 00:14:55,775
Dividing the difference by that step
gives about two point zero zero.

217
00:14:57,275 --> 00:15:01,900
And one over the cosine
of sixty degrees is exactly two.

218
00:15:02,250 --> 00:15:05,725
So the formula's slope
is the stretch we wanted.

219
00:15:06,075 --> 00:15:07,850
Two more checks.

220
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:12,488
At the equator, the tangent
of forty five degrees is one,

221
00:15:12,488 --> 00:15:16,550
and l n of one is zero, so y is
zero, as it should be.

222
00:15:18,050 --> 00:15:21,642
And at the pole, the angle
becomes ninety degrees,

223
00:15:21,642 --> 00:15:25,450
where the tangent has no end:
it grows without limit.

224
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:28,775
So the pole is infinitely far up the map.

225
00:15:29,125 --> 00:15:34,106
At eighty five degrees, y is already
three point one three Earth radii,

226
00:15:34,106 --> 00:15:38,175
about nineteen thousand nine
hundred and fifty kilometres.

227
00:15:39,675 --> 00:15:43,880
That's why Mercator maps are
always cut off before the poles,

228
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:46,500
usually somewhere past eighty degrees.

229
00:15:47,300 --> 00:15:49,975
If you skipped, here's what to keep.

230
00:15:50,325 --> 00:15:56,018
Mercator stretches north-south by the same
amount as east-west, one over cosine phi,

231
00:15:56,018 --> 00:15:59,611
so his map grows taller
and taller towards the poles,

232
00:15:59,611 --> 00:16:02,525
and it can never show
the poles themselves.

233
00:16:02,875 --> 00:16:06,875
Now we can see why a rhumb line
is straight on a Mercator map.

234
00:16:07,225 --> 00:16:11,900
A rhumb line, as we saw
in Part 6, keeps one compass bearing.

235
00:16:12,250 --> 00:16:14,750
Zoom into any short piece of it.

236
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:17,837
On the globe, it's a tiny slanted step:

237
00:16:17,837 --> 00:16:20,925
some distance west, and some
distance south.

238
00:16:21,275 --> 00:16:25,350
Keeping the same bearing means
the same slant everywhere.

239
00:16:25,700 --> 00:16:29,142
For Part 6's rhumb line
from London to Los Angeles,

240
00:16:29,142 --> 00:16:31,438
at two hundred and fifty eight and

241
00:16:31,438 --> 00:16:32,450
a half degrees,

242
00:16:32,450 --> 00:16:37,850
every piece goes about four point nine
kilometres west for each kilometre south.

243
00:16:39,350 --> 00:16:41,850
Now draw it on the Mercator map.

244
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:46,077
At each latitude, Mercator
stretches the west part and

245
00:16:46,077 --> 00:16:50,025
the south part by the same
amount, one over cosine phi.

246
00:16:50,375 --> 00:16:55,175
Stretching both parts by the same
amount leaves their ratio unchanged.

247
00:16:55,525 --> 00:16:59,626
So on the map, every piece
of the route has the same slant,

248
00:16:59,626 --> 00:17:02,475
four point nine across for each one down.

249
00:17:02,825 --> 00:17:06,475
A line whose slant never
changes is a straight line.

250
00:17:07,975 --> 00:17:10,845
The great circle keeps
changing its bearing,

251
00:17:10,845 --> 00:17:14,825
so on this map it comes out curved,
bulging towards the pole.

252
00:17:15,175 --> 00:17:19,964
The two routes swap looks: on the
globe, the great circle looks straight and

253
00:17:19,964 --> 00:17:24,375
the rhumb line curves, and on Mercator's
map it's the other way round.

254
00:17:25,175 --> 00:17:28,475
But keeping shapes has a price: sizes.

255
00:17:28,825 --> 00:17:31,075
How much is an area stretched?

256
00:17:31,425 --> 00:17:35,252
Take a small square on the globe
at sixty degrees north,

257
00:17:35,252 --> 00:17:37,575
a hundred kilometres on each side.

258
00:17:37,925 --> 00:17:41,500
Its area is ten thousand
square kilometres.

259
00:17:41,850 --> 00:17:47,275
On a Mercator map, its width is stretched
by two, and its height by two as well.

260
00:17:47,625 --> 00:17:52,003
So it's drawn two hundred kilometres
wide and two hundred tall,

261
00:17:52,003 --> 00:17:56,450
an area of forty thousand square
kilometres: four times too big.

262
00:17:57,950 --> 00:18:02,450
In general, the width
is stretched by k and the height by k,

263
00:18:02,450 --> 00:18:05,300
so the area is stretched by k times k,

264
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:08,975
k squared, which
is one over cosine phi, squared.

265
00:18:09,325 --> 00:18:14,930
At Svalbard, k is about four point nine,
and squaring it gives about twenty four,

266
00:18:14,930 --> 00:18:18,875
so areas there are drawn about
twenty four times too big.

267
00:18:20,375 --> 00:18:25,675
That's why, on a Mercator map,
Greenland looks about as big as Africa.

268
00:18:26,025 --> 00:18:31,561
In truth, Greenland covers about two
point one seven million square kilometres,

269
00:18:31,561 --> 00:18:34,575
and Africa about
thirty point four million.

270
00:18:34,925 --> 00:18:39,276
Dividing Africa's area by Greenland's
gives about fourteen,

271
00:18:39,276 --> 00:18:42,300
so Africa is about fourteen times bigger.

272
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:49,475
The Mercator map is great for directions
and shapes, and poor for comparing sizes.

273
00:18:50,275 --> 00:18:53,775
For climate maps, sizes often matter more.

274
00:18:54,125 --> 00:18:58,929
If we want to know how much of the Earth
was warmer than usual last month,

275
00:18:58,929 --> 00:19:02,500
a map that inflates the polar
regions would mislead us.

276
00:19:02,850 --> 00:19:06,328
So climate scientists
often use equal-area maps,

277
00:19:06,328 --> 00:19:09,950
such as the Equal Earth
map, from twenty eighteen.

278
00:19:10,300 --> 00:19:14,360
On it, every patch keeps its true
share of the Earth's area,

279
00:19:14,360 --> 00:19:18,825
so Greenland shows at its real
size, about a fourteenth of Africa.

280
00:19:19,175 --> 00:19:23,675
Here's the same temperature map
for September twenty twenty six,

281
00:19:23,675 --> 00:19:24,800
drawn both ways.

282
00:19:25,150 --> 00:19:30,709
The colours show how much warmer or colder
each place was than its usual September,

283
00:19:30,709 --> 00:19:34,325
the average from nineteen
ninety one to twenty twenty.

284
00:19:34,675 --> 00:19:38,350
That difference from the usual
is called an anomaly.

285
00:19:38,700 --> 00:19:42,200
On the Mercator map, the far
north looks huge.

286
00:19:42,550 --> 00:19:46,350
On the Equal Earth map, it
shrinks to its true size.

287
00:19:46,700 --> 00:19:48,725
The price now is shape.

288
00:19:49,075 --> 00:19:53,570
Towards the poles, shapes get skewed:
at sixty degrees north and

289
00:19:53,570 --> 00:19:58,275
thirty degrees east, angles can be
off by about thirty one degrees.

290
00:19:59,775 --> 00:20:04,225
No flat map can keep both areas
and shapes right everywhere.

291
00:20:04,575 --> 00:20:07,725
So the rule is: pick the map for the job.

292
00:20:08,075 --> 00:20:11,196
Use conformal maps
for winds and directions,

293
00:20:11,196 --> 00:20:14,175
and equal-area maps for comparing amounts.

294
00:20:14,525 --> 00:20:17,040
Which maps forecasters use, and why,

295
00:20:17,040 --> 00:20:21,650
has its own movie later in this
series: How weather maps are made.

296
00:20:22,450 --> 00:20:26,075
So why are there so many
coordinate systems and maps?

297
00:20:26,425 --> 00:20:28,600
There are three good reasons.

298
00:20:28,950 --> 00:20:30,775
The first is shape.

299
00:20:31,125 --> 00:20:36,150
When the coordinates match the shape
of the problem, the numbers become simple.

300
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:40,700
A ring round a hurricane's eye
is just r equals a constant,

301
00:20:40,700 --> 00:20:44,116
the Earth's surface
is just r equals its radius,

302
00:20:44,116 --> 00:20:47,675
and a great circle route
is just one angle, sigma.

303
00:20:48,025 --> 00:20:50,275
The second is measurement.

304
00:20:50,625 --> 00:20:54,525
We use the coordinates that
our instruments and data use.

305
00:20:54,875 --> 00:20:59,478
A radar sees distance and direction,
a weather station feels east,

306
00:20:59,478 --> 00:21:04,500
north and up, and weather data come
on a grid of latitude and longitude.

307
00:21:04,850 --> 00:21:06,775
The third is meaning.

308
00:21:07,125 --> 00:21:12,344
The right coordinates split a vector
into the parts that physics cares about:

309
00:21:12,344 --> 00:21:16,954
spin and inflow round a storm, along
and across a coast, or round and

310
00:21:16,954 --> 00:21:20,275
in for a whirl round
the pole, as we'll see next.

311
00:21:20,625 --> 00:21:25,825
Maps follow the same rule: we choose the
one that keeps what our question needs.

312
00:21:26,625 --> 00:21:31,975
There's one more map that every forecaster
knows: the view from above a pole.

313
00:21:32,325 --> 00:21:34,575
Let's get there from the globe.

314
00:21:34,925 --> 00:21:37,125
Here's the Earth, turning.

315
00:21:37,475 --> 00:21:41,700
Now let's rise up above the North
Pole, and look straight down.

316
00:21:42,050 --> 00:21:46,424
From up here, the circles of latitude
become rings round the pole,

317
00:21:46,424 --> 00:21:50,400
and the meridians become
spokes, like the spokes of a wheel.

318
00:21:50,750 --> 00:21:53,500
Now let's flatten this view into a map.

319
00:21:53,850 --> 00:21:56,275
The North Pole sits in the middle.

320
00:21:56,625 --> 00:22:00,202
The rings are the latitudes:
seventy five, sixty,

321
00:22:00,202 --> 00:22:03,925
forty five and thirty
degrees north, going outward.

322
00:22:04,275 --> 00:22:06,625
The spokes are the longitudes.

323
00:22:06,975 --> 00:22:11,325
Zero degrees, the prime meridian
through Greenwich, points down.

324
00:22:11,675 --> 00:22:14,059
Ninety degrees east points right, a

325
00:22:14,059 --> 00:22:19,100
hundred and eighty degrees points
up, and ninety degrees west points left.

326
00:22:20,600 --> 00:22:23,906
So going east means going
round anticlockwise,

327
00:22:23,906 --> 00:22:26,350
as seen from above the North Pole.

328
00:22:26,700 --> 00:22:28,708
This is polar coordinates again:

329
00:22:28,708 --> 00:22:32,913
the distance from the middle grows
with the distance from the pole,

330
00:22:32,913 --> 00:22:35,925
and the angle round
the middle is the longitude.

331
00:22:36,275 --> 00:22:39,438
This kind of map is called
polar stereographic,

332
00:22:39,438 --> 00:22:43,410
and this one is drawn true
to scale at sixty degrees north,

333
00:22:43,410 --> 00:22:45,900
like many weather charts of the pole.

334
00:22:46,700 --> 00:22:49,061
If you've never seen a map like this,

335
00:22:49,061 --> 00:22:53,400
it can be hard to tell where things
are, so let's take a short tour.

336
00:22:53,750 --> 00:22:56,336
To find any place, first find its ring,

337
00:22:56,336 --> 00:23:00,050
for the latitude, and then
its spoke, for the longitude.

338
00:23:00,400 --> 00:23:04,394
Near the bottom, at forty
eight point four degrees north and

339
00:23:04,394 --> 00:23:06,325
four and a half degrees west,

340
00:23:06,325 --> 00:23:11,650
is Brest: just past the forty five degree
ring, a little left of the zero spoke.

341
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:16,239
Closer to the pole, past
the sixty degree ring, is Iceland,

342
00:23:16,239 --> 00:23:20,550
at about sixty five degrees
north and nineteen degrees west.

343
00:23:20,900 --> 00:23:23,869
To its left is Greenland, the big island,

344
00:23:23,869 --> 00:23:27,925
around seventy two degrees
north and forty degrees west.

345
00:23:28,275 --> 00:23:31,625
To the right of the zero
spoke is Scandinavia.

346
00:23:31,975 --> 00:23:35,972
Further in, at seventy
eight point two degrees north and

347
00:23:35,972 --> 00:23:40,325
fifteen point six degrees
east, is Longyearbyen, on Svalbard.

348
00:23:40,675 --> 00:23:44,445
Between Scandinavia and the pole
lies the Barents Sea,

349
00:23:44,445 --> 00:23:48,425
around seventy five degrees
north and forty degrees east.

350
00:23:48,775 --> 00:23:53,775
To the right, along the ninety
degrees east spoke, stretches Siberia.

351
00:23:54,125 --> 00:23:58,782
At the top, near a hundred and eighty
degrees, is the Bering Strait,

352
00:23:58,782 --> 00:24:02,890
with Alaska to its left, around
sixty five degrees north and

353
00:24:02,890 --> 00:24:05,150
a hundred and fifty degrees west.

354
00:24:05,500 --> 00:24:09,455
And on the left, along ninety
degrees west, is Canada,

355
00:24:09,455 --> 00:24:12,750
with Hudson Bay at about
sixty degrees north.

356
00:24:14,250 --> 00:24:16,648
One last test: where's Los Angeles,

357
00:24:16,648 --> 00:24:21,375
at thirty four degrees north
and a hundred and eighteen degrees west?

358
00:24:21,725 --> 00:24:25,859
Just inside the thirty degree ring,
between the ninety west and

359
00:24:25,859 --> 00:24:29,075
the hundred and eighty
spokes, on the upper left.

360
00:24:30,575 --> 00:24:33,375
Now let's put real weather on this map.

361
00:24:33,725 --> 00:24:36,525
The air pressure falls as we go up.

362
00:24:36,875 --> 00:24:39,951
About thirty kilometres
up, in the stratosphere,

363
00:24:39,951 --> 00:24:43,155
the layer above the one
where our weather happens,

364
00:24:43,155 --> 00:24:48,025
it's only ten hectopascals, about
a hundredth of the pressure at the ground.

365
00:24:48,375 --> 00:24:50,988
The height where the pressure is exactly

366
00:24:50,988 --> 00:24:53,797
ten hectopascals isn't
the same everywhere,

367
00:24:53,797 --> 00:24:58,500
and this map shows it on the twenty
first of January, twenty twenty six.

368
00:24:58,850 --> 00:25:00,783
The colours show that height,

369
00:25:00,783 --> 00:25:05,650
and the lines join places where
it's the same, every four hundred metres.

370
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:10,600
We'll fade the place labels now, so
that the weather is easy to see.

371
00:25:10,950 --> 00:25:15,104
The surface is lowest, about
twenty eight point six kilometres,

372
00:25:15,104 --> 00:25:19,192
over the Barents Sea, at about
seventy three degrees north and

373
00:25:19,192 --> 00:25:20,775
forty four degrees east.

374
00:25:21,125 --> 00:25:23,137
And it's higher further south:

375
00:25:23,137 --> 00:25:28,100
about thirty point seven kilometres
on average along thirty degrees north.

376
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:31,725
Now let's let the air move.

377
00:25:32,075 --> 00:25:34,970
The winds blow nearly along these lines,

378
00:25:34,970 --> 00:25:38,300
round and round
the Arctic, from west to east.

379
00:25:38,650 --> 00:25:44,218
The fastest, nearly eighty metres per
second, about two hundred and eighty four

380
00:25:44,218 --> 00:25:48,800
kilometres per hour, blow over Siberia,
near sixty degrees north.

381
00:25:50,300 --> 00:25:54,577
This great winter whirl of cold
air, high above the Arctic,

382
00:25:54,577 --> 00:25:57,550
is called the stratospheric polar vortex.

383
00:25:58,350 --> 00:26:02,625
Let's describe these winds
in the map's polar coordinates.

384
00:26:02,975 --> 00:26:08,325
Pick any point, and draw its local
east and north arrows, as in Part 4.

385
00:26:08,675 --> 00:26:13,811
The east arrow points round the middle,
anticlockwise as seen from above:

386
00:26:13,811 --> 00:26:17,400
it's exactly the theta hat
of Part 1 at that point.

387
00:26:17,750 --> 00:26:21,526
The north arrow points straight
at the middle, towards the pole.

388
00:26:21,876 --> 00:26:25,576
That's the opposite
of r hat, which points outward.

389
00:26:25,926 --> 00:26:28,751
So the north arrow is minus r hat.

390
00:26:30,251 --> 00:26:35,029
That means the eastward wind u is
the tangential wind round the pole,

391
00:26:35,029 --> 00:26:39,600
and the northward wind v is the radial
wind with its sign flipped,

392
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:42,301
because blowing north means blowing in.

393
00:26:42,651 --> 00:26:47,526
So u sub theta
equals u, and u sub r equals minus v.

394
00:26:47,876 --> 00:26:50,825
Westerly winds, the winds from the west,

395
00:26:50,825 --> 00:26:55,101
go round anticlockwise,
as seen from above the North Pole.

396
00:26:55,451 --> 00:27:00,227
So the polar vortex spins the same
way as the hurricane in Part 1,

397
00:27:00,227 --> 00:27:03,917
but it's far wider:
thousands of kilometres across,

398
00:27:03,917 --> 00:27:05,726
instead of a few hundred.

399
00:27:06,076 --> 00:27:10,841
And unlike a hurricane, it isn't
fed by a warm sea: it forms because

400
00:27:10,841 --> 00:27:14,626
the polar stratosphere cools
in the long winter night.

401
00:27:15,426 --> 00:27:20,626
There's one more choice hidden in polar
coordinates: where to put the origin.

402
00:27:20,976 --> 00:27:25,101
Let's see why it matters
with a simple flat example first.

403
00:27:25,451 --> 00:27:30,130
Picture a ring of air, a thousand
kilometres from its own centre,

404
00:27:30,130 --> 00:27:33,801
going round anticlockwise
at ten metres per second.

405
00:27:34,151 --> 00:27:39,626
With the origin at its centre, every air
parcel on the ring moves purely round.

406
00:27:39,976 --> 00:27:45,451
The tangential wind is ten, and the radial
wind is zero, all the way round.

407
00:27:45,801 --> 00:27:51,401
Now keep the same air, but move the origin
five hundred kilometres to the west.

408
00:27:51,751 --> 00:27:55,226
Look at the top of the ring, due
north of the centre.

409
00:27:55,576 --> 00:28:00,626
There, the wind blows due west:
u is minus ten, and v is zero.

410
00:28:00,976 --> 00:28:05,836
Seen from the new origin, this point
is five hundred kilometres east and

411
00:28:05,836 --> 00:28:07,726
a thousand kilometres north.

412
00:28:08,076 --> 00:28:12,544
Its distance comes from Pythagoras:
we square the two legs, add them,

413
00:28:12,544 --> 00:28:14,162
and take the square root,

414
00:28:14,162 --> 00:28:18,501
which gives about one thousand one
hundred and eighteen kilometres.

415
00:28:18,851 --> 00:28:22,618
To get its r hat, we divide
each leg by that distance,

416
00:28:22,618 --> 00:28:27,501
which gives zero point four four seven
and zero point eight nine four.

417
00:28:29,001 --> 00:28:34,282
As in Part 1, the radial wind
is the dot product of the wind with r hat:

418
00:28:34,282 --> 00:28:38,390
we multiply u by r hat's first
part and v by its second,

419
00:28:38,390 --> 00:28:42,351
and add them, which gives
about minus four and a half.

420
00:28:42,701 --> 00:28:45,376
It looks as if the air is flowing in.

421
00:28:46,876 --> 00:28:49,446
The tangential wind uses theta hat,

422
00:28:49,446 --> 00:28:53,926
minus zero point eight nine
four, zero point four four seven.

423
00:28:54,276 --> 00:28:58,851
Doing the same with theta hat's two
parts gives about eight point nine.

424
00:29:00,351 --> 00:29:05,476
Same air, same wind, but the new
origin splits it differently.

425
00:29:05,826 --> 00:29:08,477
Nothing is flowing in at all: the in and

426
00:29:08,477 --> 00:29:11,726
out part comes only
from where we put the origin.

427
00:29:12,526 --> 00:29:14,701
Now back to the vortex.

428
00:29:15,051 --> 00:29:17,676
Its own centre isn't at the pole.

429
00:29:18,026 --> 00:29:21,453
It's at about seventy
two point nine degrees north and

430
00:29:21,453 --> 00:29:23,737
forty four point three degrees east,

431
00:29:23,737 --> 00:29:28,751
over the Barents Sea, about one thousand
nine hundred kilometres from the pole.

432
00:29:29,101 --> 00:29:32,892
Let's draw a ring two thousand
kilometres from the pole,

433
00:29:32,892 --> 00:29:36,479
split the wind at each point on it
into its round and

434
00:29:36,479 --> 00:29:39,525
in and out parts
with the pole as the origin,

435
00:29:39,525 --> 00:29:42,301
and average round the ring, as in Part 1.

436
00:29:42,651 --> 00:29:45,928
The round part averages
only about seventeen and

437
00:29:45,928 --> 00:29:48,658
a half metres per second, and the in and

438
00:29:48,658 --> 00:29:51,458
out part is typically about thirty three,

439
00:29:51,458 --> 00:29:55,076
almost twice as big:
a ratio of about one point nine.

440
00:29:56,576 --> 00:30:02,426
Now draw a ring two thousand kilometres
from the vortex's own centre instead.

441
00:30:02,776 --> 00:30:07,953
The round part averages about forty
seven metres per second, and the in and

442
00:30:07,953 --> 00:30:10,369
out part is typically about twenty,

443
00:30:10,369 --> 00:30:13,751
less than half: a ratio
of about zero point four.

444
00:30:15,251 --> 00:30:18,990
So about its own
centre, the vortex is mostly spin,

445
00:30:18,990 --> 00:30:21,776
just like the hurricane round its eye.

446
00:30:22,126 --> 00:30:25,842
Choosing the origin is part
of choosing good coordinates:

447
00:30:25,842 --> 00:30:28,776
put it at the centre
of the thing that turns.

448
00:30:29,126 --> 00:30:31,823
When the polar vortex weakens or splits,

449
00:30:31,823 --> 00:30:35,801
cold air can spill south into
the places where people live.

450
00:30:36,151 --> 00:30:39,905
That story has its own
movies later in this series:

451
00:30:39,905 --> 00:30:42,776
Waves on the jet, and The polar vortex.

452
00:30:43,576 --> 00:30:47,501
To sum up, flattening the Earth
always stretches something.

453
00:30:47,851 --> 00:30:52,673
The flat square map, with x equal
to the Earth's radius times lambda,

454
00:30:52,673 --> 00:30:55,190
and y equal to the radius times phi,

455
00:30:55,190 --> 00:30:58,265
is true along the equator
and the meridians,

456
00:30:58,265 --> 00:31:01,760
but it stretches
east-west by one over cosine phi:

457
00:31:01,760 --> 00:31:04,276
about one and a half times at Brest.

458
00:31:04,626 --> 00:31:09,551
Scale factors like this matter whenever
we measure slopes on a weather grid,

459
00:31:09,551 --> 00:31:14,476
where one degree east at Brest is only
seventy three point eight kilometres.

460
00:31:14,826 --> 00:31:18,120
Mercator stretches
north-south by the same amount,

461
00:31:18,120 --> 00:31:22,601
so small shapes and angles stay
right, and rhumb lines are straight.

462
00:31:22,951 --> 00:31:26,629
His map's height is the Earth's
radius times l n of the

463
00:31:26,629 --> 00:31:30,173
tangent of forty five degrees
plus half the latitude,

464
00:31:30,173 --> 00:31:31,645
which puts Brest about

465
00:31:31,645 --> 00:31:36,326
six thousand one hundred and sixty
seven kilometres above the equator.

466
00:31:36,676 --> 00:31:39,470
But areas grow by one over cosine phi,

467
00:31:39,470 --> 00:31:42,778
squared, about twenty
four times at Svalbard,

468
00:31:42,778 --> 00:31:46,601
so equal-area maps are
better for comparing amounts.

469
00:31:46,951 --> 00:31:52,751
Seen from above the North Pole, a map
is polar coordinates again: east is round,

470
00:31:52,751 --> 00:31:57,826
north is in, and the polar vortex
is mostly spin about its own centre.

471
00:31:58,176 --> 00:32:01,842
With that, our seven parts
on coordinates are complete,

472
00:32:01,842 --> 00:32:05,976
from arrows on a flat map
to winds over the whole round Earth.

473
00:32:06,326 --> 00:32:10,503
Next in this series comes the gradient:
how fast, and in which

474
00:32:10,503 --> 00:32:14,276
direction, temperature
or pressure changes across a map.

475
00:32:14,626 --> 00:32:17,935
It's where the one over
the Earth's radius times

476
00:32:17,935 --> 00:32:21,176
cosine phi that we met
here does its real work.

477
